{"name":"Clear Vision","hex":"#e7f0f7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"clear","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Transparent in colour.","Bright; luminous; not dark or obscured."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["All the way; entirely.","Not near something or touching it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clear"},{"term":"vision","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The sense or ability of sight.","Something seen; an object perceived visually."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To imagine something as if it were to be true.","To present as in a vision."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vision"}],"entries":[{"title":"Clear Vision","blocks":[{"text":"Clear Vision is a 2007 tactical shooter video game developed by the Swedish developer Eldring Games and was for a period the most downloaded game in the Android Appstore."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clear_Vision"},{"title":"Transparency and translucency","blocks":[{"text":"In the field of optics, transparency is the physical property of allowing light to pass through the material without appreciable scattering of light. On a macroscopic scale, the photons can be said to follow Snell's law. Translucency is the physical property of allowing light to pass through the material. It allows light to pass through but the light does not necessarily follow Snell's law on the macroscopic scale; the photons may be scattered at either of the two interfaces, or internally, where there is a change in the index of refraction. In other words, a translucent material is made up of components with different indices of refraction, while a transparent material is made up of components with a uniform index of refraction. Transparent materials appear clear, with the overall appearance of one color, or any combination leading up to a brilliant spectrum of every color. The opposite property of translucency is opacity. Other categories of visual appearance, related to the perception of regular or diffuse reflection and transmission of light, have been organized under the concept of cesia in an order system with three variables, including transparency, translucency and opacity among the involved aspects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Dichroic_filters.jpg/330px-Dichroic_filters.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transparency_and_translucency"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Backlit_mushroom.jpg/500px-Backlit_mushroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Backlit_mushroom.jpg/1280px-Backlit_mushroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Backlit_mushroom.jpg/500px-Backlit_mushroom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Translucency of a material being used to highlight the structure of a mushroom","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABacklit_mushroom.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/1D_normal_modes_%28280_kB%29.gif/250px-1D_normal_modes_%28280_kB%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9b/1D_normal_modes_%28280_kB%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/1D_normal_modes_%28280_kB%29.gif/250px-1D_normal_modes_%28280_kB%29.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Normal modes of vibration in a crystalline solid","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1D_normal_modes_(280_kB).gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Laser_in_fibre.jpg/500px-Laser_in_fibre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Laser_in_fibre.jpg/1280px-Laser_in_fibre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Laser_in_fibre.jpg/500px-Laser_in_fibre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A laser beam bouncing down an acrylic rod, illustrating the total internal reflection of light in a multimode optical fiber","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALaser_in_fibre.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Silica_core_fiber_minimum_attenuation.jpg/500px-Silica_core_fiber_minimum_attenuation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Silica_core_fiber_minimum_attenuation.jpg/500px-Silica_core_fiber_minimum_attenuation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Experimentally measured record low attenuation of silica core optical fiber. At 1,550 nm, wavelength attenuation components are determined as follows: Rayleigh scattering loss ~ 0.1200 dB/km, infrared absorption loss ~ 0.0150 dB/km, impurity absorption loss ~ 0.0047 dB/km, waveguide imperfection loss ~ 0.0010 dB/km.[14]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilica_core_fiber_minimum_attenuation.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Expl0469_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg/500px-Expl0469_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Expl0469_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg/1280px-Expl0469_-_Flickr_-_NOAA_Photo_Library.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&u